It is January the 27th, 1997. My name is Hermann Hirschberger. I'm interviewing Thea Rudzinski. Her name is Sonnenschein. The location is London, England. And the language is English. Could you please give me your full name and spell it?

TR: Thea Rudzinski, T-H-E-A- R-U-D-Z-I-N-S-K-I.

INT: Did you have any nicknames?

TR: No.

INT: What is your Hebrew name?

TR: Taube.

INT: And what is your date of birth?

TR: 28th of August, 1927.

INT: And what is your age?

TR: 69.

INT: And place of birth?

TR: Vienna.

INT: In Austria?

TR: Yes.

INT: What is the name of your father?

TR: Josef, Josef-Leib.

INT: And your mother?

TR: Jetti [? Rachel. ?]

INT: What was her maiden name?

TR: [? Schletlinger. ?]

INT: Do you know the birthplace of your parents?

TR: My father was born in [? Huschatten. ?] And my mother was born in-- it's called Monastyryska. I think at home they used to call it Monastrishtsh. But I think it's called Monastyryska.

INT: And is that in Austria?

TR: Poland.

INT: In Poland. What did your father do for a living?

TR: We had a grocery store, grocery, green grocery. After some years, we sold perfumery goods, and funnily enough, paraffin. Now how that was allowed to be with a grocery store, I'd never know.

INT: Some groceries.

TR: Yes.

INT: And do you remember your grandparents?

TR: I remember my grandmother on my father's side very well. Because she was still alive when I left Vienna. My grandfather died when I was about two or three.

My other grandparents, my grandfather died. They lived in Poland. My grandfather died before I was born.

And my grandmother I knew for a very short while. When I was about three years old, my mother went for a visit to Poland. And she took me back. And we must have been there for about four or five weeks.

INT: And do you remember going to Poland when you were three years old?

TR: I don't remember going to Poland. But I remember coming back. Because we went for a visit to my grandmother in Vienna. And my brother was with me.

And as children, we weren't allowed to have a knife. And he picked up a knife. And I forgot my German in Poland. And I came back speaking Yiddish.

And I said [YIDDISH]. And everybody started laughing. Because it was comical a little girl was speaking only Yiddish. So that part I remember when I came back.

INT: So that was at a very early age?

TR: I was three.

INT: Do you remember the journey to Poland?

TR: No.

INT: Did you go by train?

TR: Yes. Not by air, certainly, so it must have been train.

INT: Mhm. And it was just your mother and--

TR: My mother and myself.

INT: And your grandmother in Vienna, were you-- did you have close relationship with her?

TR: Yes. Very close, very close. She used to come to our shop once a week, always brought my brother's favorite sweets and my favorite sweets. And on Shabbos, my father used to daven in a very early minion. So he used to come home and make kaddish. And we had breakfast.

And then we used to go-- he likes to go to a shul where there was a chazan, so we went there. Or sometimes we went to my grandmother's, or we went in the afternoon. I saw her at least once or twice a week.

INT: She lived within walking distance?

TR: Yes. Yes. We all lived in Zweite Bezirk.

INT: Mhm. And was your grandmother Orthodox like--

TR: Yes. Yes.

INT: --your family?

TR: Yes.

INT: Traditional, with--

TR: Yes. Yes. Very Orthodox.

INT: [INAUDIBLE]?

TR: Yes. Yes.

INT: Uh huh. And your grandparents on the other side were equally--

TR: Yes.

INT: --Orthodox?

TR: Yes.

INT: So the ethos of your home was strictly Orthodox.

TR: Yes. Yes.

INT: Can you tell me, you've already said you remember the journey, any other very early childhood memories?

TR: Of when I went to Poland?

INT: No, any in Vienna?

TR: When you say childhood memories, you mean as a-- really as a little girl?

INT: What's the earliest you can remember?

TR: Went to kindergarten, to school. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Even as a little girl, helped in the shop as much as I could.

INT: Do you recall going to kindergarten?

TR: Yes.

INT: At age four?

TR: Oh, I must have been about four, five. I think school started at seven on the continent.

INT: Was that Orthodox kindergarten?

TR: It was a-- yes, an Orthodox kindergarten. I think it was a private kindergarten, somehow or other.

INT: What can you recall about those days?

TR: What can I recall? I remember we had a lot of twins in the kindergarten. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Two were-- two teachers and some of the friends I had in kindergarten we carried on through school together for as long as I stayed home.

I'm still friendly with one now. She lives in America. We correspond. Otherwise, I don't remember too much about kindergarten.

INT: And you went to school?

TR: Yes. Jewish school.

INT: Tell me a bit about your Jewish school.

TR: I went to a local Jewish school. Actually, there was only one local Jewish school. And then when I came to high school, I went to the [? Hias ?] gymnasium. But there I was only for a year. Because then, when Hitler came into Vienna, it closed up. So I went--

INT: [? Hias ?] gymnasium was Orthodox?

TR: It was a Jewish one-- not really. It was a very mixed crowd-- a very mixed crowd.

INT: And did you go to cheder apart from going to the gymnasium?

TR: Yes. Yes. Then I went to-- I went to cheder already when I went to the Talmud Torah school. But when I went to [? Hias, ?] I carried on with the cheder.

We had some Jewish studies in Hias gymnasium, but of a different-- of a different kind.

INT: When you went to cheder, you must have been, what, five years old?

TR: No, a bit older, I think. Well, I think that started when school started, when about seven or eight.

INT: And which year was that, approximately?

TR: I was born in 1927. So it would have been 1935, something like that.

INT: At that time, did you realize anything at all politically?

TR: No.

INT: Or--

TR: Too small.

INT: --antisemitism?

TR: No.

INT: You were not aware of any feelings against Jews?

TR: No. The only thing I do remember, but I don't know whether it had anything to-- it must have been political. I had a cousin who lived with us. And he was studying to be a doctor.

And he lived with us. And I remember one day he came home with his head all bandaged up. So it was something to do with students. And I think it was political. But I have no idea what.

INT: Did your parents ever-- obviously not-- did not speak about it?

TR: No. No. No. There was not much-- there was no time for discussion about politics. They had a shop. And they were both in the shop.

And they had a Dienstmadel, what would be called an au pair, to help to look after us. And it was-- there was just no time.

INT: Were your parents in the middle class or above position?

TR: No, I would say in the middle class or a little bit below.

INT: What's-- you-- you've got one sibling.

TR: I had one brother, yes.

INT: Yes. Which year was he born?

TR: He was born in 1924.

INT: So he's three-- he was three years older.

TR: Years older.

INT: Do you recall playing with him and having fun with him?

TR: Not really so much. I really had friends more girls my-- you know.

INT: What was the language which was spoken at home?

TR: Mainly German, bit of Yiddish. And when my parents wanted to say something we couldn't understand, they spoke Polish.

INT: But did your parents belong to a Hasidic environment or a [INAUDIBLE] environment?

TR: I would say Hasidic.

INT: Did they-- what sort of synagogue did they attend?

TR: They went to a shtiebel. Actually, when I say 'they,' my father, mainly, my mother was not a shul-goer. Yontif, she used to go maybe Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur. But--

INT: Do you remember the name of the shtiebel?

TR: [? Seder ?] Gerrer Klaus.

INT: And was the-- was it the [INAUDIBLE] Gerer Rebbe? Or was that just the name of the--

TR: It was just the name of the Klaus. The shtiebel was called that. I don't know where the [? Seder ?] Gerer Rebbe was then. I really don't know.

INT: Did you accompany your father to synagogue?

TR: It was very near to us. So I used to go afterwards myself a little bit. You know, it was small. Children used to play there as well.

INT: Can you tell-- say a bit about the social environment of your home, Shabbat and festivals?

TR: Ah, yes. Well, we-- we kept Shabbos and Yontif, very traditional. Shabbos afternoon, I used to go what was [INAUDIBLE], which is equivalent to [INAUDIBLE] here. We used to come together, girls meet and sing together, sometimes dance, somebody told the sidra. That were on Yontif as well.

I used to go to my grandmother's house. It was a great meeting place. And my father was the old-- my father was the oldest of eight, that I remember. I think there were even more. But I remember eight.

And there weren't many married yet at that time. My father was the older one. Then, slowly, all the others started to get married. On Shabbos afternoon, it was a known place. Everybody came to my grandmother, or to some cousins of my father, and so on. It was the general meeting place.

INT: Your father's siblings lived in Vienna?

TR: Yes.

INT: And the environment in which you lived, did that permit you to meet non-Jewish people?

TR: I didn't have any cause. There was really no cause to meet non-Jewish people. Because all around us lived Jewish people. There lived some goyim as well. But I mean, we really have no connection with them.

The house below, [INAUDIBLE] the house was a non-Jewish lady. But she was-- she actually was very good to us. Because in Hitler's times, I remember once the Nazis knocking on the door. And the door was very weak. It needed just a good bash. And she shouted the equivalent of they're not at home.

INT: I see.

TR: So in a way, she was quite good to us, this--

INT: This house-- house--

TR: House [INAUDIBLE] yes. Her name was [? Lochner. ?]

INT: How many people lived in the house?

TR: How many people lived in the house? Oh, it was a big house. I have no idea. But I mean, on the floor that we were there must have been about six people on each floor. And there were quite a few floors.

INT: Did you have modern conveniences?

TR: No, anything but.

INT: Telephone?

TR: Not even water [LAUGHS] in the-- in the flat.

INT: Really?

TR: Yes. We had one-- one bedroom. And there were four of us, my parents, my brother, and myself. There was another room, which was called a cabinette and that even had the wardrobes in, because the bedroom wasn't big enough to hold wardrobes because it had to have sleeping accommodation for four. And I don't know where the Dienstmadel slept.

INT: But you--

TR: I remember a folding bed. She must have slept in the cabinette, I think. And then a kitchen, and the shop was adjoining. And behind the shop, there was a little magazine.

INT: Did you have-- you had running water, though?

TR: Not in the flat, on the-- out-- on the passage outside.

INT: And that's where the toilet was?

TR: Yes, correct.

INT: So you had to leave your apartment if you wanted to go to a certain place?

TR: Yes. Yes. Which was dark and dreary.

INT: Mhm.

TR: And the water was brought in in a big white watering can, and a-- and I had a bucket. And it was a special [INAUDIBLE] sort of thing that you put on-- on the bucket.

INT: Was this-- this was in the late '20s and early '30s.

TR: Well, since I remember.

INT: Did you live in a popular part of Vienna?

TR: Yes, a very Jewish part, [INAUDIBLE].

INT: The city?

TR: A little walking distance-- it's difficult for me to judge that. Because I was only a little girl. And although I've been back, I was back too short to really know how far it is to walk there.

INT: And do these memories, are they fairly real memories of the apartment, the furniture you had?

TR: Yes, very real. Very real. I could paint each one out now.

INT: Can you remember--

TR: Better than what I could-- what I remember from here.

INT: Do you recall what possessions your parents had in terms of furniture?

TR: Yes. 100%. I remember-- I remember we had a beautiful tea service, a beautiful tea service. Nowadays maybe it wouldn't be called so beautiful. But in those days, it was.

And my mother had it on a beautiful tray. And it was standing on top of the wardrobe. And I always used to say, why don't you take it down to use? So my mother said, when you grow up and you bring your husband home, that's when I'll bring it down. It was not to be.

INT: And what about books, Jewish books?

TR: Yes. We had some Jewish books.

INT: Did your father have a shas?

TR: No.

INT: A Talmud?

TR: No.

INT: Uh huh.

TR: We had the [? Humashim, ?] you know, the general thing more use. People didn't have money for that sort of thing. We didn't have money for that sort of thing. I think it took him all his time to make a living.

He worked very hard, got up early in the morning to go and get-- we sold kosher milk. So he had to go to the [INAUDIBLE] to get the milk and to-- and to deliver it early in the morning.

In fact, we used to go. My brother used to. Although we lived in Zweite Bezirk we had a few customers who wanted kosher milk in Neunte Bezirk that was, like--

INT: Yes.

TR: --right near it. So before school-- school started at 8:00-- my brother went to one place and I went to another place to deliver milk before we went to school.

INT: But who supervised the milking of the milk?

TR: No. That was brought from a dairy where there was somebody who supervised.

INT: Ah. They had their own supervisor.

TR: Yes. Yes.

INT: I see. Did your apartment, which seems to be a-- you've described as reasonably elementary, did it have electricity?

TR: Yes.

INT: And how was the cooking done?

TR: Well, to begin with, I remember something that you had to heat with wood and so on. But after that, I remember there was what you called the [INAUDIBLE]. That was just the top, no oven.

If you wanted to bake a cake, you baked a cake and you took it to the baker. And he put it in his oven. And then you went to collect it.

INT: Was the [INAUDIBLE] fired by gas?

TR: Yes. Yes. In fact, you did the same thing for [INAUDIBLE]. You made the [INAUDIBLE] and you took it to the baker. And he gave you what you would call now a cloakroom ticket.

And on Shabbos, you went with your cloakroom ticket. And sometimes, if somebody had the same pot as you or made a mistake, you came home with the wrong [INAUDIBLE]. So you went back again. [LAUGHS]

INT: You said you took your cloakroom ticket. Was there an eiruv in Vienna?

TR: Yes. There was.

INT: An eiruv is a part of the city where you're permitted to carry--

TR: Yes.

INT: --on Shabbat. Did you have a radio in your apartment?

TR: No.

INT: Did your father read newspapers and--

TR: Can't remember. I don't think he had time.

INT: Your mother was a housewife, but helped in the shop.

TR: No, and helped in the shop a lot.

INT: Tell me, what do you recall about going to the-- what was the name of the school, [? Hias? ?]

TR: [? Hias ?] gymnasium.

INT: [? Hias ?] gymnasium.

TR: Very difficult. I found it very difficult. We learned Latin, which is a difficult language. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I found it hard, but I-- I managed.

INT: Did you have social activities which are noteworthy?

TR: Not really. Didn't have time, in any case. We used to came-- come home from school, cheder, homework, a little bit of play with my friend, and that was it.

INT: The Jewish population of Vienna, was that fairly large--

TR: Yes, I think so.

INT: --as a percentage of the city?

TR: I think so. Was quite a lot of Jews in Vienna. Don't remember numbers.

INT: No. But the percentage of Orthodox?

TR: Was quite large, I think, at least where I lived in Zweite Bezirk.

INT: At that early time in your life, do you recall going on holidays with your parents?

TR: On holidays with my parents-- I went with my mother sometimes to the country. Because we had a shop and my father had to keep the shop open. Went to-- not far away, like [INAUDIBLE], or you know, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] places that were fairly near.

INT: Tell me--

TR: Once I remember going with my auntie on holiday. She went. And she had two children. So I went with her on holiday.

INT: Tell me a bit about your brother.

TR: He was three years older than I. He went-- I don't remember which school he went to in elementary school. But he went to [? Hias ?] gymnasium as well. So he was there much longer than me, must have been three years longer.

He was very talented with painting and artistic work. So when Hitler came into Vienna, he must have been then 14. And my parents thought maybe they were going to send him away, maybe he should have some sort of a little bit of a profession in his hands. And they sent him for a course for sign writing, which he did. And we had sort of an extra counter in the shop, and he used to practice on there.

And when he came to England, this was his profession to begin with. He was a sign writer. So it stood him in good stead.

INT: Was the other artistic talent in the family?

TR: Not really. My father had beautiful handwriting, very beautiful handwriting. And he could freelance do beautiful Jewish letters, beautifully. It was something, I think, which he inherited from him, because my brother also had very nice handwriting.

But later on, he was in business together with a non-Jewish fellow. And it didn't present any problem. Shabbos and Yontif was closed. But then it came to a pitch when this non-Jewish fellow wanted to have the business open one Shabbos. And that presented problems.

So my brother got out of the business.

INT: Yeah.

TR: And--

INT: He must have been fairly young at that stage. So--

TR: He got married fairly young. He was in this business together with this non-Jew for many years. And when he got out of the business, he did what he was-- what was really very befitting for him to do, became the registrar of marriages for-- in the chief rabbi's office.

And that was-- he was made for this job and this job was made for him. He put everything in that office. Everybody-- if I ever phoned there and I mentioned his name, he put everything into order. And he was a very, very methodical and orderly person.

INT: We'll speak a bit more about that later.

TR: Yes.

INT: Let me ask you, in 1938, when Hitler invaded Austria, it was March the 13th, 1938. You were 10 and 1/2 years old.

TR: Yes.

INT: What do you recall about that?

TR: I don't remember too much of when he invaded it straightaway. But I remember soon afterwards this frightened-- everybody was frightened. And they took men. And they painted the streets. And they took girls that they should scrub away in the streets. Because I remember some of my aunties were taken.

And some things we used to purchase for the shop local. And my father was frightened to go out. And my brother, they didn't want to send my brother.

But I was blonde, so I didn't look very Jewish. So they used to send me. It was a whole-- very frightened, everything was a frightened atmosphere.

INT: That was within days of Anschluss?

TR: Yes. Yes. And this frightened business and everything went on right till when they closed the shop.

INT: What created the fear, in your mind?

TR: That everybody-- everybody was frightened. And everything that went on around you made you frightened. If there was a knock on the door, you were frightened who was at the door. I mean, everything.

And then, I think I remember that they closed the shop down. Because they closed all the Jewish shops down.

INT: Within the short time--

TR: Yes.

INT: --of Anschluss?

TR: Yes.

INT: Closed down temporarily or permanently?

TR: No. Closed it down.

INT: Do you recall-- that means that was your first contact with antisemitism as it existed in Nazi Germany?

TR: Yes.

INT: Before that, life in Austria was reasonable.

TR: For me, as a little girl. I don't know how it was for a grown up person.

INT: I understand. When Hitler had invaded Austria, did your parents show any alarm?

TR: Yes, definitely.

INT: Did they plan to do anything?

TR: Yes. We tried to see how we could get out. But I remember my father-- what did he send-- my brother to the library, bring home books, like address books from other towns, like Peru and Chile. And we looked up the name Sonnenschein.

And we wrote away. My name is Sonnenschein. I'm in trouble.

Maybe you're a relative of mine. Maybe you can send us an affidavit. And this went on to lots and lots of places. We wanted to get out.

And I think when this Kindertransport came up, my parents registered my brother. But I wasn't due to go on it. And then, like a little girl, I must have thought my-- my brother was going on some fun journey. I said [INAUDIBLE], and so on.

So the gentleman, this was a Dr. Schonfeld transport. And the gentleman who arranged this in Vienna was-- was Mr. [INAUDIBLE] [? Scheinfeld ?] And his daughter was my very best friend. So I had a little bit of pretext there, at least my mother did.

So she went there. And my mother must have realized that although I cried like a little girl, but she realized maybe it would be a good thing to send me away. So I got into that transport in the last minute. And when I got to Holland, my papers weren't finished. I have to stay there for a few days till my papers were finished.

INT: This Dr. [? Scheinfeld, ?] who was a personal friend--

TR: Yes?

INT: --was he an official of the Kindertransport organization?

TR: I don't know. I think he had something to do with the Kultus Gemeinder or something. I'm not very sure. But he was a Mr. He wasn't a Dr. [? Scheinfeld ?] and very, very nice man.

And their home was really a little bit of my second home. Because their daughter was my best friend. And either she was by me or I was by her. If we had to go a little errand or something, she came for me or I came for her. We were very, very close.

INT: But the Kultus Gemeinder was the overall community, not the Orthodox--

TR: Yes.

INT: --branch?

TR: Yes.

INT: Mhm. And how did you-- had you at that stage heard of Dr. Schonfeld?

TR: No. Nothing whatsoever.

INT: Did you have any idea at all in the first months after the invasion what was due to happen to you?

TR: No.

INT: Did you have any feelings about Austria or staying in Austria?

TR: No, none whatsoever. I was too small.

INT: But at that stage, you were nearly 12.

TR: No, it was 11. I was 11 in August. And I left in December. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

No. It really didn't-- for me, life was just my home and going to school and going to cheder and to the group of girls, you know, not political at all, anything. Just the only thing, when Hitler came, is just it's frightening. I remember always being afraid.INT: We were just saying that at that stage you were only 10 and 1/2 years old. And after Hitler invaded Austria, and the time between March and December, when you left, was not a very eventful time.

TR: No.

INT: And you didn't really have much idea of what was due to happen to you?

TR: No. No. No idea at all.

INT: Do you think your brother knew?

TR: Maybe a little bit more than me. Because I think from 11 to 14 is quite-- you have already a bit more sense.

INT: Yes. Yes.

TR: And the fact that he went to learn a profession that my parents had in mind in case he left home.

INT: Are there any anecdotal occasions, any occasions which you remember which are worthy to speak about during that time in connection with the Hitler regime?

TR: Only as I said before. I remember them taking people out of their houses to scrub the floors. I don't know what they'd painted on the floors, probably Mogen David. And then they told the Jewish people, the girls, and quite grown up girls, girls of about 17, to scrub it away, and young men.

And that one day I remember, when they came to knock on the door. And my mother said to us, we should all get into bed. And we got into bed.

And we were more or less pulled the covers over our heads. And then, when I heard these big bangs, I heard the house [INAUDIBLE] and shouting, they're not in, which was really very fortunate for us.

INT: Do you by any chance know what that special occasion was?

TR: No. I think they just knocked on-- they knocked on doors. They wanted to take all the men out, I think.

INT: Was it to do with Kristallnacht?

TR: I don't think so. I don't think so.

INT: Did you know what Kristallnacht was?

TR: I didn't at the time, properly. I have known a bit more about it since.

INT: So in the protected environment in which you lived, life just went on?

TR: It went on, not the same. For instance, I didn't go any more to my [? Hias ?] gymnasium. I went-- in fact, I don't even think, at the end, whether I went much to school. Because I think we were-- everybody was afraid to go in the streets. I don't even remember if I went to school.

Just-- and our shop was closed. And it was-- food was not quite as plentiful. It was-- it was a different life. You felt there was something different. And there was.

INT: Yes.

TR: And everybody was looking for a way of how to get out of Vienna.

INT: At that stage, or only after Kristallnacht?

TR: Oh, I can't answer that. I really don't remember.

INT: Of course, Kristallnacht was on the 9th to the 10th of November.

TR: November.

INT: And you left--

TR: And I left at-- I left about four-- six weeks later.

INT: In December?

TR: No. I would say already before.

INT: What do you recall about preparations for emigrating and going to England?

TR: No preparations at all. I only found out a couple of days before I went. You just take a-- I took-- first of all, nobody had the kind of belongings that people have nowadays. Everybody had one week-day dress and one Shabbos dress and little bit of underwear. You just packed it in one case. And that's how I came.

INT: The-- the journey from Vienna, was that by train?

TR: By train, yes, to-- to Holland and then cross the channel.

INT: Did your parents come with you part the way?

TR: My mother took me to the station. That's the last I saw of her.

INT: At that time, you had no idea that that would be--

TR: No.

INT: --the last?

TR: No idea.

INT: Before we commence on your journey to England, can you tell me what happened to your mother later?

TR: Well, we corresponded to Vienna. And then one day, my father wrote that my mother's gone away. And I think they just tried to get out illegally, smuggle themself.

I have correspondence from Lyon. I have correspondence from Antwerp, from my mother, from my father, from different places. And then, after a long time, then she was in Camp de Gurs in the Pyrenees. I think I used to get Red Cross messages. And that-- that was it.

INT: Let's get back to your departure from Vienna in December 1938. Your mother took you to the station.

TR: Yes.

INT: What do you recall about the journey from there?

TR: It was Hanukkah. And I remember one of the boys lighting a Hanukkah lamp. It was the fifth Hanukkah light, on the train. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Ironically, I don't know what the right word is, my brother died on the fifth Hanukkah light. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Traveling, we stopped-- we stopped somewhere once. I remember the Nazis coming in once and just looking and going out. And once we were somewhere when they brought us in a drink. And I think it must have been already an English cup of tea. And we just couldn't drink it, because we weren't used to drinking tea with milk.

And then we came to Holland. And in Holland, we were taken to a-- like a little transit camp, et cetera. That must have been on a Thursday, I think.

Because on the Friday night, they said we're going further on the journey except a few of us. And I was one of the few, just about a handful whose papers weren't ready. And I remember there was uproar, uproar in the [INAUDIBLE]. Because they didn't want to go because of Shabbat.

And somebody came along and said, you have to go. Because we have to make more room for more refugees coming. And it's not normal times. And it's-- you have to go. And of course, the transport carried on. But we were left there for about a week. And then we went--

INT: Because of the Shabbat?

TR: No. We were left there for a week because my papers weren't finished.

INT: Ah, right.

TR: Yes.

INT: Right.

TR: So I-- I went the following week with a few-- I knew a couple of people who were left with us at the time.

INT: I see. Was your brother with you at that stage?

TR: No. Yes. But my brother went with the transport on Friday night because he had his papers.

INT: I understand. Understand. Tell me what happened then, your journey to England.

TR: We traveled by boat. And we arrived-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I don't know where it was. Must have been Dover or somewhere. And, again, we went to a transit camp overnight.

And then after that, we put-- a few of us went on a train. And I remember they put a luggage label on us, into the hole of the lapel of the coat. And I kept on looking at this and reading it. Mrs. Ellison, 380 Roch Valley Road. Rochdale Road, you know? But I was reading it the German way.

And we got out of the station. And we were all in a waiting room. And this lady came to claim me.

INT: Where was your brother then?

TR: My brother was in England. I didn't-- I didn't know exactly where he was at the time. I think he came to a hospital first.

INT: Did he come with Kindertransport?

TR: Yes. Yes. Yes.

INT: Well, how did you feel? I mean, you were 10 and 1/2 years old.

TR: A bit weepy, a bit homesick. But that's about all. The lady I went to was very good to me.

She was a widow. And she had five daughters, the youngest of which was 17. Her husband had died. And she had a business in Manchester.

And they must have advertised people who wanted to take children to save them. And she-- she was a very kind lady. And she must have put her name down for a girl. And that was me.

INT: And did she pick you up in--

TR: She picked me up at the station in Manchester.

INT: Yes.

TR: And took me home. It was very strange. Because I knew a few words of English, but very few words. And they-- I mean, her daughters spoke only English.

She knew a few words of Yiddish. We had to get by. I learned my English very quickly, forgot my German completely. And we have--

INT: Did you ever find out why this lady was involved with you, personally?

TR: Because she--

INT: She asked for a girl?

TR: Yes. She asked for girl, because she had five daughters. So a boy would have felt out of the place there. And she was just a kind-hearted lady, a Jewish lady.

She put her-- she saw adverts. There they advertised for people who would be able to take in refugee children. So she answered this advert.

INT: Were her children able to adapt to you?

TR: They were all a lot older than I was. The youngest was 17. And then there was quite a gap between-- I think there was about six years between that one and the next one. And the others were much older. But they were very good to me and very nice to me and tried to understand me as best as they could.

INT: Were they-- was it an Orthodox family?

TR: No, not-- not at all. Not at all.

INT: This must've upset you?

TR: Yes. It did. It was in a very non-Jewish district. Blackley is the name. The only thing Jewish in that district is a cemetery. The Jewish cemetery is in Blackley, which was just up the road from where we lived. But that's about all.

INT: Yes. How long did you live there in Blackley?

TR: 3 and 1/2 years. I went to an English school, a big girls' school. I was the only Jewish girl there.

There was one teacher who took a great interest in me. I think she must have read a lot about what went on. And she had a little bit of a soft spot for me. And I remember she invited me home to her house once, very nice. But they were very nice to me at school.

INT: Did you maintain your orthodoxy when you left?

TR: To begin with.

INT: Yes.

TR: To begin with, but as time went by, I dropped most of it. Because it was very difficult. You know, a child can only persevere-- I think even for a grown up it's hard. But I think for a child it's even harder. I persevered and persevered and persevered. But at the end, I'd more or less--

They kept kosher to a certain extent. She bought her meat from a butcher from a district who delivered from a Jewish district. And she kept fleishik and milkhik separate. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] That part was not quite-- was quite good. But not-- she-- she lit candles on Shabbat, but put the radio on next to it. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

But very-- they were very nice to me. I can't say one bad word. She was like-- she really tried to be like a mother.

INT: Like a mother. So you felt comfortable there? Or did you miss your parents?

TR: Comfortable is not the right word, I think. I don't know. I'm trying to think if I would have come to a family who would have been similar if they would have been Orthodox, I might have felt a bit more comfortable.

INT: Which part of Manchester was this?

TR: Blackley.

INT: Blackley. Blackley.

TR: And only a bus ride away from an Orthodox part. But I never went there. Because-- oh, I'm not telling the truth. I did go there.

Because one of the daughter's had her hairdresser in the Orthodox part. It was a little bit of a relative of theirs who were Orthodox. So whenever she went to have her hair done, usually on a Sunday, she took me with.

Now when I came to this house, I felt much more at home. Because it was a bit more like what I was used to at home. But apart from that, I really had no connection with the-- anything that was Orthodox. Because I was too small.

INT: Did you see your brother?

TR: No. Not at all. We just corresponded. He-- he at that time, he was in a hospital in London.

INT: Yes.

TR: And then he was in a hospital in Cardiff. And after about 3, no, 3 and 1/2 years it was-- he-- it seems funny when I think about it. We didn't even speak on the telephone. I don't think we ever lived there was a telephone. No, there wasn't a telephone.

And it's not like nowadays, you pick up a telephone or something. We didn't speak. We used to correspond.

And once he wrote he was coming to visit me. And when he came, he saw-- you see, he remained Orthodox. Because he was in a hospital with Orthodox boys. And then he went to the Cardiff hospital, which was Orthodox.

And he saw how I'd become. And he didn't really care for it very much. So-- and I think I'd outgrown my stay there. Because as long as they took a little refugee girl in, it's fine.

INT: Yes. Yes.

TR: But once you became a big girl, it wasn't the same anymore. So I think he must have suggested to them, don't quite know whether he suggested or what, he would see if I could come to the hospital as well. And that's what happened.

INT: Yes?

TR: Yes. But when I went to the hospital, she came to visit me there, to see if I was happy. One of the daughters came to see me, if I was happy. She was very, very caring.

INT: Was she upset that you left?

TR: Mixed feelings.

INT: And you kept in contact for--

TR: Always.

INT: --a long time?

TR: Always. I'm still in touch with two of the daughters who are alive. They-- they've moved from there. There was a bit of a rift between these sisters. She had five daughters. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The youngest was in the land-- in the land army in the war. And where she was, near Manchester, she met a Jewish soldier from America. And she married him and she went there.

The oldest one got married after some years. She was no youngster. And the other three never got married.

And I think they became a bit frustrated spinsters. And a jealousy came amongst them. And the mother and the oldest sister fell out with these three.

And I was sort of-- I had nothing to them, and they were good to me, and they were good to me. For me, it was very awkward afterwards. But I somehow remained with-- very good with all of them.

In fact, she always-- when I got married, she came to my wedding. And after I had my-- Chajim, my oldest, she came here to see if I needed-- you know, when she used to come and when she came to stay by me, she looked around the house. And like-- like a mother, she would go into the bathroom and see there was a little dish for soap missing or something. The next thing you knew, she went out and she bought one, really very, very caring.

And she always used to save-- she didn't have too much naches from her children. Because the three daughters didn't get married. And the only one that had children was this youngest one who married to America. But unfortunately, her husband died at a very young age. I think it was only about 30.

And she used to say, oh, she only wanted to live to my son's bar mitzvah. And it was just, I think about a year before, or half a year before, she used to belong to a Friendship Club. And they used to go on holiday. And she went with this Friendship Club to Westcliff.

And one Friday evening, there was a knock on my door. And there's a policeman standing there. Are you Mrs. Thea Rudzinski? Yes.

Mrs. Ellison, that was the lady, she's on holiday in Westcliff. So I said, yes, I know.

Anyhow she took bad. She had some sort of a hemorrhage. And she was asking for me. Well, I was a little bit of a predicament. Had it been an ordinary day of the week, I'd just picked myself up and--

INT: --go.

TR: --go. So I sent my husband to the rav here to ask what I should do. So he said, if it would be that you could save her life, by all means. But if not, say a message that as soon as Shabbos is out, you'll come there.

Well, I did that. But I was very uneasy. Because she was going to have an operation.

I made my Chajim-- and he was wasn't bar mitzvah yet. I made him-- no, I'd arranged with this police that-- I sent this message back. And I said if-- when she's had the operation, would you please phone through. And my Chajim took the phone off. And they said she didn't live through it.

And I'm sorry to this day. That I shouldn't have asked the [INAUDIBLE]. I should have just picked myself up and gone. Because she really was good to me. And she asked for me more than any of her daughters.

INT: She was good to you both in supplying a home as well as supplying a mum?

TR: Yes. She was very good to me. I mean, the 3 and 1/2 years that I lived with her, she clothed me and fed me. And-- or at school, you didn't need to pay for school. It was a public, ordinary school.

So-- but she was very good to me. So-- so were-- so were her daughters. They were all nice to me.

I can't say that any-- sometimes I used to think now, if I was in the same position, whether I might have been jealous that my mother would take in. And they weren't jealous of me, not at all. They were very, very nice to me.

INT: Were there other people like that in Manchester or in Blackley?

TR: Yes. Because this-- she had a business. And on Wednesday afternoon, the business closed. And she-- she had a hardware store.

And she went to town to do some shopping for her store. And when she took me with, she told them who I was. One went out and bought me a watch. One came back with a box of chocolates. I still have a brush and comb set that one gave me. Oh, they were all very nice to me, very nice.

INT: It's nice that you maintained contact.

TR: Yes. Yes. When it was the 50 years that I came to England, I sent them flowers. And now I-- I mean, I speak to them during the year on the telephone a few times.

But when it comes to any sort of anniversary, I came to them. I make over the special phone call, you know? They're too old. There's only left, the--

INT: Too old, the sisters?

TR: --the one that lives in America still, well, with her, I haven't had much contact. But the-- two of the others are alive. And they're both well in their 80s.

INT: During-- how long all together were you there?

TR: I think 3 and 1/2 years.

INT: 3 and 1/2 years.

TR: Yes. Don't remember dates well.

INT: Yes.

TR: But it was just less than four years, definitely.

INT: Yes. And did you go to school?

TR: Yes.

INT: Which school?

TR: [? Crossley ?] Senior Girls' School. It was a big school in Manchester, and quite near to me, about 10 minutes' walk.

INT: And did you do anything to maintain your Jewish studies, which had been inculcated by your parents?

TR: No facilities whatsoever. My Jewish education stopped at 11. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I had my English education there, which was not high school standard, ordinary school standard. But there was-- I don't know whether the English children are a bit lazy or what.

But within six months, I knew English perfectly. Within a year, I was top of the class in all the exams. And I wasn't really a sluggo for studying. It-- I don't know. I just took my schooling more serious, where the English girls just, you know?

INT: When you first stayed there, you must have spoken English. Because they couldn't speak.

TR: No. Couldn't speak. I knew table. I knew chair. But I couldn't put a sentence together.

INT: Can I just speak about those-- during those days and the contact you had with your mother?

TR: When I forgot my English, I used to correspond with my brother. And I used to have to send him a letter written in English to my parents, used to have to translate it to me, send it back, and [? then I ?] sent it back home. But that correspondence, I don't think, went on for very long because then my parents didn't have a proper address.

INT: But did they live in Vienna at the time?

TR: They did-- for to begin with, they did. Because I remember my father kept on saying maybe you could get a-- a permit, like a domestic permit for my mother. And she gave me where to go.

And I didn't go there. But I remember I phoned this lady. Because this was in the Jewish part, I remember. And I phoned her and I explained to her. And she helped me to get a permit.

Had this permit arrived two, three weeks before, my mother may have still had a chance. But it came too late. And the war broke out.

So that I must have done still when I was 11. Because the war broke out the following year, right, in 1939. I was there about nine months before the war broke out. I still have this permit upstairs.

INT: Did you-- when war broke out, did it mean anything to you?

TR: Yes.

INT: Or--

TR: Yes. That I do remember. I think I remember it was a Sunday morning when they declared war on the radio. And it made a lump in my throat, you know?

INT: Did you understand the meaning of the war with--

TR: No, not--

INT: --with Hitler's Germany?

TR: --I didn't really understand. But it-- it did something to me. As little as I understood, but it did something to me.

INT: And the lady you lived with, she was understanding at that time?

TR: Yes. Yes. Yes.

INT: Mrs.--

TR: Ellison.

INT: --Ellison?

TR: Yes.

INT: Tell me what do you know about the enforced separation between your parents during those years.

TR: Very little. I don't-- if I did know, I've forgotten it. I don't think I knew too much.

INT: Did your father write separately?

TR: Yes, I think after a time, yes.

INT: Did you then realize your mother had-- was not free any longer?

TR: I didn't know where she was. My father said he also didn't know where she was.

INT: What sort of effect did this have on your life?

TR: I still didn't quite realize 100% what was going on.

INT: You were too immature?

TR: I don't-- I don't really think that when it came-- till the end of the war and everybody turned up from concentration camp, I don't know if the general public-- we here had an idea. They knew a little bit.

I mean, but I don't think they realized it was what it was. I mean, during the war, I don't ever remember hearing that-- that Jews were being gassed or something. I don't know. I don't-- I don't think anybody really realized till afterwards, people came and told you what went on.

INT: But what did you find out after the war happened to your mother? She was taken to Camp de Gurs.

TR: I just didn't hear anything. We had Red Cross-- Red Cross letters. And the fact that we didn't hear anything, just you know, after the war, all these lists appeared of people here and people there. And everybody went running for lists and seeing if [INAUDIBLE].

INT: So--

TR: I did find out-- I can't remember. Must have been after the war that I found out that my father was in Switzerland. I don't remember if we found out before the war ended or after the war ended. I really don't remember that.

I know we spoke to him on the phone. So it's more likely as not that it must have been when the war was finished. Because I don't think there was telephone communication during the war with Switzerland.

INT: I don't know. There might have been, but--

TR: I don't think so. I think it was after the war was finished that we really found out.

INT: Yeah. But you don't know in which year your mother left Camp de Gurs?

TR: No.

INT: You've never heard that?

TR: If I have, then I don't know. I have some mail upstairs. If I would search through it, and-- I tell you, I don't really-- every year, when I make Pesach, I take this bundle of correspondence out. And sometimes I say put it away, don't look at it.

Because when I take it out, I can't leave it. And I'm crying. And I'm miserable. So sometimes I just take it, put it out, and dust it, and put it back again.

INT: How did you become aware of the ultimate fate of your mother?

TR: Well, we never heard anything after the war. So we realized that she must have been killed like so many millions of others. It was only when, some years back, my brother went to Yad Vashem. And every time there were new papers.

And then he found some papers which showed exactly which day she was sent to the gas chambers.

INT: Deported from--

TR: Not deported--

INT: Camp de Gurs?

TR: Yes. [INAUDIBLE] was.

INT: But in the meantime, before that, the Red Cross maintained correspondence with you?

TR: Not really.

INT: No?

TR: Not really. We're just like-- like everybody. You didn't hear anywhere. They didn't think so. They weren't alive anymore. What exactly happened with her, I didn't know. Not till my brother found this-- these notes in Yad Vashem.

INT: During those years with the good care of Mrs. Ellington, did you-- was there a tendency, being young, to forget your parents?

TR: No. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Not to forget them. More vague than when I first came, but not to forget them.

INT: But it did not set up a conflict in your mind?

TR: No. No.

INT: Tell me about your schooling, subsequent to the arrival, you know, your first tuition. What did you do during the war?

TR: During the war, when the bombing started, and the schools also closed. Some children were evacuated. I didn't want to be evacuated. And I don't think Mrs. Ellison was keen on me being evacuated.

Sometimes I was in school, a couple of hours here, a couple of hours there. In fact, I must have written this back home. And I have letters upstairs which my mother or my father wrote to me and said, if you're going to have only-- through all the troubles that they were going, they still had in mind-- if you're only going to school a couple of hours, [GERMAN].INT: --maiden name Sonnenschein and this is tape number three. It was your feeling that your mother was under the impression that your education was being neglected in the early stages. But in fact, this was wartime Britain.

TR: Yes.

INT: What happened after 1940-41? Did you start work?

TR: Well, I left the Ellison place, it must have been-- I came in December '38-- '39, '40, '41-- it must have been in the middle of '42 when I must have gone to Cardiff to the hospital to be with my brother. I looked for a job. And I got a job in a warehouse, earning all of 10 shillings a week, 50 pence, I believe. And I enrolled in evening classes to learn shorthand typing. And as I learned my shorthand typing, I was promoted to the office from my warehouse work. And I did quite well there. I think I got a whole pound a week.

And we stayed in this hostel. It was actually a boys hostel, but when I came to it a lot of the boys had gone abroad, they found relatives. And my brother had met his future wife, because her mother was the cook in the hostel. And she came to this hostel and all her children were-- came to England from-- they came from Baden bei Wien. And they were all by non-Jewish people. So as she became the cook there, she slowly took her children, one by one, to the hostel. And my sister-in-law was one of her children. And they met and had some understanding amongst them.

So the hostel closed down soon after that. Because it wasn't worthwhile for the community to keep it open because, as I say, all the boys had gone. And there was just the [? Schmall ?] family-- that was my sister-in-law's family, and just a few of us. So wherever my sister-in-law's-- future sister-in-law's family went, we lived with them and we moved with them. So they moved to London, so we moved with them to London.

INT: But who arranged in 1942 for you to go to the hostel in Cardiff?

TR: My brother.

INT: Were you happy to leave Mrs. Ellison?

TR: Mixed feelings. Mixed feelings, because I grew very fond of her and her daughters. And I didn't know what I was in for in the hostel yet. But on the whole, I was quite happy there.

INT: Was it traumatic for you to change?

TR: No, not at all, not at all.

INT: Of course at that stage, you were only 15.

TR: Yeah-- no, I found the change not difficult. I found this job and evening-- I went to evening classes. And fell back into a bit of the old pattern of life. And--

INT: Into old Orthodox patterns.

TR: Yes, yes.

INT: But did that mean anything to you?

TR: I was very happy to take it back again.

INT: Did you have a bad conscience about your previous years?

TR: I do sometimes, but I had no alternative. I really don't-- I'm trying to think if I know anybody who lived with-- in the circumstances that I lived and-- it was next to impossible to-- it was next to impossible. I couldn't possibly have. Certain things you can, but you just-- you could do it maybe for three months or something, but not-- not for three and a half years.

INT: Coming back to Cardiff now, you did mention that-- that the hostel soon closed--

TR: Yes.

INT: --after you got there. Did Dr. [? Scheinfeld ?] have some dealings with that hostel?

TR: I really don't know.

INT: Was Dr. [? Scheinfeld-- ?]

TR: I think it was-- I think the Cardiff community upheld this hostel. I only got to know of Dr. [? Scheinfeld ?] after that.

INT: At that stage you didn't know about Dr. [? Scheinfeld. ?]

TR: No, but I knew that he-- I don't know if I knew at the time or afterwards that I came under his auspices.

INT: How did this happen that you came under his auspices?

TR: Because this whole transport--

INT: Oh, the-- the

TR: --that I came on--

INT: --the original 3

TR: --was-- yes, the original Kindertransport was under his auspices. Because it was arranged by the Agudat.

INT: Do you know what happened to some of the other children on your children's transport? Did they also go to Manchester too?

TR: Yes, some went to Manchester. Very few went to families. I remember one girl, she lives here, going to a family. She was very unhappy there. And I think she left soon after.

INT: Because of Orthodoxy?

TR: I don't know. I don't know if it was-- it must have been partly that.

INT: Tell me what happened when the hostel closed in Cardiff.

TR: I moved with my brother, and his future in-laws, and their-- their other children, to North London, to Evering Road. And in the meantime, I had an uncle here as well in London, but I hadn't seen-- seen him either. So when I came to London, that was the first time I'd seen him. And I'd been in England already over four years.

And he got me a job. Now, actually, was at my first job? No, in London, I think I had another job before that. My shorthand typing was getting better so I joined here Pittman's College in the evening, and I had an office job, and went to work, and lived with my brother's future in-laws.

My brother still wasn't married because they were hoping the war was going to be finished soon. And that-- we didn't know my mother yet-- that hopefully, my parents would come over and then they'd get married.

But then as it came nearer towards the end of the war, lots of people came to my sister-in-law's mother, said it's not right, they're engaged for such a long time, you should let them get married. So they got married in 1945, in August. But my father wasn't-- yes-- no, my father was not here. My father didn't come till I think the following year.

INT: Did you-- after 19-- you-- you were at-- the hospital closed about 1942 and you moved to Evering Road in London, did you, after that, '43, hear about your mother being deported from camp de Gurs?

TR: I don't think so. I don't think so. We were-- we were bombed out a couple of times, went back to Manchester. Went back to Manchester.

INT: The whole family.

TR: The whole lot.

INT: Yes.

TR: Wherever they moved, I moved.

INT: The sister's in-law's parents.

TR: Yes, wherever I moved, my brother and I moved. They sort of adopted-- we were their-- like their children.

INT: Was there any inkling at all what happened to the Jews in Europe?

TR: No, I don't think so. I don't think so.

INT: And do you think with your brother there was an inkling, being three years older?

TR: Maybe, I don't know. Can't answer that. I really don't know.

INT: And you worked in London on and off?

TR: Yes, I went to Manchester. And then I had a job there again. And then when the house was put to rights again in London, came back again to London.

INT: To Evering Road.

TR: Yes, yes, back to Evering Road.

INT: And the [INAUDIBLE], the sort of environment in London, was that Dr. [? Scheinfeld ?] environment, or?

TR: Yes, that was an Orthodox environment. And then when I came back here, I knew then that I was on the Dr. [? Scheinfeld ?] transport, and I remember I needed help with something once, I didn't have money for something, I remember when I went up there and they were very good. They helped me with whatever I needed. There was-- I don't know if you remember Rabbi [? Pelz ?] was in the office there, a very nice gentleman.

Yes, they were very nice-- very nice. I didn't need-- I had no great longings, but sometimes I must have needed a help with something. I don't remember what it was.

INT: Did you meet Dr. [? Scheinfeld ?] personally?

TR: Yes, yes, on several occasions.

INT: Tell me a bit about the reunion with your father.

TR: He-- he came, it must have been about a year after the war when he came. It was-- I looked very much like one of his sisters. Her name was [? Rosa. ?] So, he hadn't seen me for all those years, so he kept on calling me [? Rosa. ?] I remember it was-- it was-- there was something missing, but the lost years.

INT: How did he get to England?

TR: By air.

INT: Under what organizations?

TR: Harry Goodman helped me to get him over.

INT: Agudat Yisrael?

TR: Yes, they helped me to get him over. We were living in Manchester at the time. Or were we? Yes.

INT: And you heard from him as soon as the war--

TR: Yes--

INT: --finished.

TR: I mean, we-- we phoned. We phoned. I don't know how we got to know, but-- I don't remember exactly, but--

INT: Did you then live with your father?

TR: No, my father-- when he came, I was living with my sister-in-law's parents, but my sister-in-law and my brother were married. So he went to live with them. They had a little flat--

INT: With the young couple.

TR: Yes, they had a little flat in Manchester and he went to live with them.

INT: And you were in London, of course, at that stage.

TR: No we were In Manchester.

INT: No, you were in Manchester.

TR: We were in Manchester at that stage.

INT: Yes, yes, not in Evering Road.

TR: We were-- no, Evering Road was London.

INT: Yes.

TR: We were in Manchester. We lived then in Bignor Street, and my sister-in-law had a flat in-- and my brother, in Smedley Lane. It wasn't-- just five minutes up.

INT: What impression did you have when you met your father again? By which time you were 19.

TR: No, I wasn't 19 yet. I must have been younger than 19. [PAUSES FOR 10 SECONDS] 17, 18-- was I 19? I don't think I was 19 yet-- maybe. What--

INT: 1927 through '46.

TR: What-- what impression--

INT: Did your father make on you after all that long separation?

TR: I felt the years missing. It wasn't the same like when you grow up with a father.

INT: Did you initially feel some estrangement?

TR: Yes, a little bit. A little bit.

INT: Did he feel the same?

TR: He didn't say so in as many words, but I think he must have done. He must have done. I mean, it was a long time. It's not like a half a year or a year.

INT: But it certainly didn't make too deep an impression.

TR: No, no, no, we-- after a little while, we sort of got used to each other again.

INT: What did he do when he came to England? Was he--

TR: He came to England and he was always a man who liked to work and not sit around. And he looked around for what he could do. And he got a job-- there was a textile place in Manchester, [INAUDIBLE], owned-- the [INAUDIBLE] family, which was quite well known. And he worked there.

And then after a time, my brother's in-laws wanted to go-- after-- you know, we went back to Manchester being bombed out. They wanted to go back to London. So my brother and sister-- I don't remember how long afterwards that was. I think it came to a stage already before we moved that my father decided he'd like to settle down again. And he was introduced to my-- became my stepmother afterwards.

And when we all moved back to London, he decided to stay there. He stayed in Manchester. I'm trying to think with whom he lived. I don't even remember. I must check that one. And shortly-- and we were in London. And shortly after that, he married this lady. And they set up home in Manchester.

And they had three children. And when the-- they were very small still, the youngest of them was eight months, he died of cancer. So my stepmother was left with these three children to bring up.

INT: When did your father--

TR: My father remarried in Manchester.

INT: Yes, when did he pass away?

TR: Oh, that's a long time ago now.

INT: Yes, yes.

TR: I was married maybe three years--

INT: Right.

TR: --or something.

INT: Right, yes.

TR: It's-- it's-- it's over 40 years.

INT: Yes.

TR: I think it was in--

INT: Yes.

TR: Could it be--

INT: Anyway, your stepmother--

TR: --53?.

INT: --was left with the three--

TR: With the three children to look after. And I got married in the meantime, while my father was still alive, after he was here for a couple of years. And my brother-- she-- she went to work, my stepmother. She was a cook and she went to work in Manchester, in Hillel House. And she managed to get somebody to look after the children during-- while she was out at work.

When it came to yontif, she came either to my brother or to me, to London. If we went on holiday, we never went on holiday unless we took them with. One year my brother took them with, one year we took them with. If we couldn't go away and take them with, we didn't go either. And--

INT: And the three children, in other words, your step siblings.

TR: Yes, they're all married now.

INT: In Manchester?

TR: No, one in Manchester, one here, and one in Gateshead. They have their own families. One of them-- two of them have already some children, married. And we're in touch all the time. It's not the same as my other brother, I must be very honest. I think it's-- they three are closer than they are with me as well because it's different-- I am old enough to be their mother. So it's a-- it's a different relationship. But we are-- we keep very close. We come to each other's--

INT: Your stepmother, is still alive?

TR: No, she died, it must be a couple of years ago. And there's a little story to it again. I don't know what to call it, not coincidence. We never had a yahrzeit for my mother, till my brother found that out. We kept Zion Adar, Moshe Rabeinu's yahrzeit, always kept it, all the years.

And when my brother came back from [INAUDIBLE] with these papers which said the 18th of Elul, he went to ask [INAUDIBLE] what yahrzeit shall we keep? And the rov said, as you have now a date, please keep the new date. So we dropped Zion Adar. When did my stepmother die? Zion Adar. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] It's a little bit more to it, no?

INT: Very interesting, very interesting. It certainly left an impression on you.

TR: Yes, definitely, it's-- I think it's-- it's-- it's higher than we can see or understand.

INT: Tell me a little bit about your family-- your children.

TR: When you say my family--

INT: Your children.

TR: My children. Well, we married in 1950. I'd-- I'd come-- I'm just trying to think. We moved-- we moved to London then. And when my father was married for about a year, he says what about coming to Manchester. And I was at a restless age. I didn't find anybody to get married to here, so I thought, oh, what have I got to lose, so I'll go to Manchester.

So I went there and I lived there for about-- oh, it must have been maybe the best part of a year. And then I was introduced to my husband. I think he actually-- he came to-- I think I came here. I came to London on a holiday to my brother and my sister-in-law and I was introduced to him.

And I went back to Manchester and he came to see me there. And then we got engaged and we decided when we were getting married. And as I didn't have anybody-- really, my father was busy with the little children and trying to make a [? panosa-- ?] a living, I didn't have any-- really anybody to do anything for me for my preparations for getting married, to find me a flat or anything.

So I decided I better pick myself up and I better move to London a bit before I got married. So I picked myself up and I went to live with my-- my sister-in-law and my brother lived with-- with her parents. They always lived together and got on wonderfully well. So I moved there as well.

INT: Evering Road.

TR: Evering Road, yes. And I came and I got a job. And looked for a flat and found a hall, and all the-- all the different preparations that there are. And we got married in 1950. I lived-- I lived on Evering Road, but they had a flat across the road. And it wasn't easy times.

I went back to work after I got married and my husband had his job. We didn't earn very much, but we-- we managed to scrape through. When I think back now, I remember when I got married, my father said-- [GERMAN] 200 pounds I have, I give it to you with the greatest of pleasure. Do with it whatever you can. And I thought, 200 pounds, that's quite a lot of money in those days. I don't know how much it would be an equivalent nowadays.

So it was-- this flat I got was on the top floor somewhere. I had a bedroom, a dining room, a kitchen, and another little spare room. So I had-- but it was empty. But a bit of liner was lying on the floor, not in the kitchen, in the other rooms. So somebody told me where you can get the bedroom suite wholesale, so we went to buy that. And then I went round, looking for a dining room, and I found somewhere a cheap one.

And I remember I bought a table second hand. I still have it downstairs. And my brother helped me paint the legs of it, it should look nice. And we bought four chairs and we managed. I remember my father worked in textiles, he brought me home sheeting, and I cut it, and I made my own bedding. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

And then, in 1951, my son was born. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I stopped working for a time. And when he was a little bit bigger, I went back to work. He went to kindergarten and I went back to work. And then in 1954-- or '51? No, it must-- in 1955, my second son was born. But then I didn't go back to work anymore. And I was living in this flat still under great hardship.

Well, nowadays nobody would want to live like this-- no hot water, no bathroom. For my son, no toilet use. Oh, it was-- the landlady was a Jewish lady. I don't like to talk about, she's not alive anymore. But if I came in on a rainy day and came in with my pram, I used to rush upstairs and before I'd done anything I went down with a [INAUDIBLE] to wipe it, I shouldn't leave her floor dirty.

It was driving me mad. I said to my husband, if we don't find somewhere to live, I'll end up in a lunatic asylum. My children weren't allowed to walk on the floor. The minute I came in I had to have soft slippers. They couldn't play with a toy that made a little bang or something. It-- you can't control children so much, healthy children. So we decided, well, we'll just have to get out somehow.

We had no money. How do you buy a house without money? And I think I had 150 pounds. So my husband saw this house, went to an agent and he saw this house. And I just didn't care where I was moving. I said it's a house, it's got four walls, just-- just let's go-- I can't. But how do we get the deposit?

So I borrowed from one society a couple of hundred pounds. And I had some aunties in Australia and an aunt in America, and I wrote them of my hardship. And each one sent me 100 pounds. And we had a deposit. And we moved in this house. We had really just the minimum down, the absolute minimum. We lived for four years of floorboards. I used to scrub the stairs every week for four years.

INT: Was your-- had your daughter been born by then?

TR: She was born in 1958. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] That's a short long story. Nowadays, it's not very uncommon. Then it was very uncommon. She was a placenta previa birth that nearly cost me my life. I was-- I was very lucky to be alive. We-- when we moved in this house, so we had to pay a mortgage, which was all of 3 pounds something a month, but we didn't have it. So we slept in one bedroom and I let all the other rooms out.

And in a way, that was my miracle. Because I had the room let and this-- this birth makes you hemorrhage and I just screamed out. And he heard me scream. And he managed to get hold of my husband. Otherwise, if they would have been-- come back, I'd have been gone. So, anyhow, we're here to tell the tale.

INT: Absolutely, absolutely. Tell me a bit about your husband. You met your husband and he was a survivor.

TR: Yes.

INT: What did this mean to you, having come here--

TR: It didn't mean anything to me at the beginning, nothing whatsoever. I met him, I liked him, he liked me and that's all there was to it. I don't even remember if he told me about it to begin with. It really meant nothing to me at that time. It was only later on as I got a bit older that it began to mean something to me. And when he talked about it, because when we first met he didn't talk so much about it. It's only after we'd been married for a few years and as the children came along, he always talked about it, you know.

INT: To you?

TR: Yes, to-- you know, as the children got bigger, on Pesach, for seder, always talked about it.

INT: Did he himself-- was he affected by his experiences, as far as the marriage was concerned?

TR: Not really. Not really. He had his difficult points, but I think any other person can have difficult points in some ways without having been through the Holocaust. I can't say that any of them were really because of the Holocaust.

INT: But as you said, it all came back later.

TR: Yes, I think it's-- now, when they've got more-- and especially now, there's so much Holocaust activity going on, because everybody realized that these are the youngest members who survived the war. And listen, everybody's getting older. And it's--

INT: When was the '45 Society formed?

TR: Oh, I don't know when, but many years ago-- many years ago.

INT: Did-- did you-- you-- you were active in the '45 Society.

TR: Really, the only active part that I've really taken was with the Sefer Torah. Then I was very active. Apart from that, not. My husband goes. I support him in anything that he needs doing that, but with the Sefer Torah, I was very active, because it--

INT: Could you just say a couple of words about the '45 Society because we know about it from a previous interview.

TR: Well, the-- it was formed from all the boys who came in 1945. And they really have a very strong bond between them because of what they went through. They keep very close together, immaterial of their beliefs or anything. They're just-- They're just a bunch of very nice-- very nice fellas, all of them.

I mean, I don't agree with everyone, what everyone-- a lot of them have married out, a lot of them, their children have married out. The ones that are married out, I can see as the years go by, the majority of them are feeling it. Because I think their non-Jewish-- their non-Jewish partners are leaving them slowly.

The ones whose children are married out are very upset, because no matter how not religious they are, they still want their children to be Jewish. And I think it upsets them very much when they marry out. But I suppose when they mix in such ordinary circles, it's-- it's bound to happen.

But they're all very, very nice and they keep together. They come together once a year for this reunion or if they make a simcha, they always-- always-- the families are always-- either all the boys, or a group of boys, or the group that come from their town, or--

INT: I understand.

TR: Yeah, it's-- I think it's very good. It's-- it's-- they need it. They need it, to come together. And when they come together on their reunion, they forget their wives there. They're all busy sitting together and all talking about old times. And I feel that's very good for them. I think it's part of the therapy for them.

INT: But in recent years, they've become more and more aware of their past.

TR: Yes, I think so. Because they realize that the years are going by.

INT: I understand.INT: Can you perhaps tell me a little bit about how your family developed in relationship to your and your husband's strong Orthodox beliefs?

TR: We always send them to Orthodox school. The boys, when they finished the Orthodox school, went to yeshivas. My daughter went to Manchester Seminary after she finished school.

We've always seen that they're mixed in Jewish environment, because I think that's the only way that you can keep your children Orthodox. Once you send them into a different circle where they come across non-Jews, or Jews which are not Orthodox, it's very easy to be led away. So we've sort of always seen that their friends were Orthodox Jewish boys and Orthodox Jewish girls. And it's very important to us.

INT: And their own children live in the same tradition?

TR: Yes. 100%. I find, in fact, their own children have become more Orthodox than we are. They've become more aware. As we are not so chassidish, they've become more chassidish. And they like that way of life. And if that's what they're happy with, then we are happy with it.

INT: And do you find the same thing applies to your stepmother and her three children?

TR: Yes. Yes. She also brought her children up very frum, and they're all very Orthodox. Yes. And the one-- the children of theirs, which are married, are also Orthodox.

INT: Yes. Yes.

TR: And my brother's children and [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] grandchildren and great-grandchildren.

INT: Can you just say what happened to your brother?

TR: My brother was the registrar of marriages in the chief rabbi's [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] office. He needed a heart bypass, and, unfortunately, didn't come around. He had Dr. [? Yacub, ?] the famous man.

But we knew that he had a problem. Because when he was a young man, he got blood pressure. And they took him into hospital for a couple of days to do several tests. And the tests showed that he must have been born with a hole in the heart.

Fortunately, I think it's fortunately, or that's how God wanted it, they didn't say at that time do any operations. He just had to live with tablets and medication. But it got to such a stage that he couldn't live anymore just with medication.

He-- he just-- it was impossible. And he had to have this operation. And he never came around. He was 62, a very young man, and left a-- a very devoted wife, an exceptionally devoted wife, and five lovely children, who, thank God, were all married.

INT: Has she remarried?

TR: He saw-- no. She would never remarry. She still-- she-- it's now over 10 years, and she still hasn't got over it. She still hasn't come to terms with it. Listen. They met--

INT: You're in close contact?

TR: They-- very close. They met in the hospital when they were 16. You know, it's a long-- it's just-- no. She would never consider marrying again. I mean, she has got [INAUDIBLE] a bunch of-- she has got a lot of grandchildren and great-grandchildren [INAUDIBLE]. She has a lot of naches from them all.

But nothing makes up to her. She-- when she goes to a simcha, you can see she's not enjoying it. She's just there because she has to be there. But it's-- to her, life without my brother is not life. Which, in a way, I can understand.

INT: Tell me what contact you have maintained with Kindertransport.

TR: I'm a member of the Kindertransport. I get their correspondence. I look through it. I don't always write in all these-- they tell you to write in your simchas and all the other things, and write letters. I'm not a very great letter writer of all these things.

I went to the reunion. If they'll have an-- as much. But last time, they sent a thing saying they want to make another one. Would I come? So I said, all being well. I don't usually go to their Hanukkah parties, because it's always a time when I make a Hanukkah party, or I have with my friends. But--

INT: All round, although your life was saved by Kindertransport, it seems that you have acclimatized very well to your new home in England.

TR: Yes. I think so. I think so.

INT: Yes. Is there anything else you would like to say regarding your transition to another country and your marriage to a survivor?

TR: My-- as I said before, I really, at the time, didn't realize what it was to marry the survivor. It's come up a little bit of problems during life. But I think there could have been problems even if he hadn't been a survivor. I mean, all sorts of problems somehow crop-- crop up.

I can't see me ever moving from here. My husband would have liked to have moved to Israel. He loves Israel. He feels, which I can understand, because he was a survivor, he says when he comes there, he says, I feel free. And I don't feel the same thing here.

He has a sister there, which is a-- a lot. And he likes to see her. And he also realizes that she's not getting any younger, and he's not getting any younger. That's why he likes to go as often as he can.

I like Israel for a visit now and then. But I don't think I could live there. I don't. The climate doesn't agree with me. I don't like the hot weather.

And I have my children here. So-- and it's a-- it's a bit late in life to start getting used to new. Here I have my friends. I have my social activities, my-- whatever I do, I have here. I don't think I could start that all over again somewhere else.

INT: Having gone through the life you have, and seen what you have seen, is there, in conclusion, a-- a message or a thought you would like to express?

TR: A message? I don't know. Maybe a message about marriage. I feel that if-- when you get married, if you want to be happy, it's a case of give and take. You can't always expect the other person to do all the giving or all the taking. And, thank God, I see my children, Baruch Hashem, happy and my grandchildren. And I hope that's how it'll continue.

INT: Thank you very much.

Would you like to tell us about this photograph?

TR: This is my paternal grandmother. Her name was [? Ettie ?] Sonnenschein. She lived in Vienna. And I think this picture must have been taken either in 1937 or 1938.

INT: Thank you. Who are these nice, little kids?

TR: That's my brother, Joshua Sonnenschein, and myself, Thea Sonnenschein. It must have been taken round about 1929, or maybe 1930, in Vienna.

INT: Thank you.

This picture is?

TR: This picture is of my parents, Josef-Leib Sonnenschein and Jetti Sonnenschein. Her name was [? Yente ?] [? Ruchel, ?] but she was called Jetti at home. My father was called sometimes Leo. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] This must have been also taken round about 1938, in Vienna. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] That was my father. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And this is my mother.

INT: Thank you.

What is this document?

TR: This is a shul ausweis card, which I think we must have needed for going swimming or attending other things, probably at a reduced price, or something like that. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I don't know when it was taken. Probably in 19--

