TR: This picture was in the Manchester Guardian,a couple of days after I arrived in England, which is 19-- December 1939. I'm not quite sure whether it was 27th, 28th, or 29th December.

Mrs. Ellison was the lady-- the lady on the right is who adopted me. And then I am with a luggage label. That was my address where I was going to. And the other girls next to me were girls which were on the same transport, and also came to Manchester. I think some of them have gone to Canada since.

INT: And December '38?

TR: Yes. December '38.

INT: Interesting. Thank you.

Who is this young couple?

TR: This is my husband and myself. This is our engagement picture, in 1949.

INT: Where was--

TR: My husband-- my husband being Israel Rudzinski, from Piotrków. And my name is Thea Rudzinski, nee Sonnenschein, from Vienna.

INT: And where was this taken?

TR: This was taken in London.

INT: Very good.

This is?

TR: This is my oldest son, Chajim, and his wife, [? Hanchi. ?] She's from America. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] They got married in--

INT: 1972.

TR: --1972, in New York. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Before, when they got engaged.

INT: Thank you.

Would you like to say something about this lovely couple?

TR: This is my second son, Josef [? Yuheda ?] Rudzinski, and his wife, [? Sima. ?] Her name is [? Rosenblum, ?] from at home. She's from Holland. And they got married here, in 1977. And they live here.

INT: And the wedding was in London?

TR: Yes.

INT: Can you speak about this picture, please?

TR: This is my youngest child, my daughter. Her name is Miriam-Rachel. And she married Noah [? Abramchik ?] from Zürich, from Switzerland. Their wedding was here in London. And she got married in 1978.

INT: And where do they live now?

TR: And they live here--

INT: What does he do?

TR: --in London. He's in the jewelry business. All my three children are in the jewelry business. But not together. And doing different things.

INT: Yes. And his hat denotes that he is a Gerrer Hasid.

TR: He is a Gerrer Hasid, yes. Yes. My boys are Bobovers. And he's a Gerrer.

INT: Thank you.

Would you speak about this photograph?

TR: Can we not get a little bit more of him in? Because this-- [PAUSES FOR 9 SECONDS] this is my brother, Joshua Sonnenschein. He was also known as Rebbe Sonnenschein. He was the secretary and the chief of marriages, in the chief rabbi's office. And unfortunately, he passed away 10 years ago. It's-- at this point, maybe something to say. We left Vienna on the fifth Hanukkah light, and he passed away on the fifth Hanukkah light.

INT: Very poignant. And he was--

TR: He was--

INT: He was a well-known man in London.

TR: Yes. He was very well-known. Anybody who got married during the time that he was a secretary, knew him very well. Because he was very pleasant to deal with. If you met him once, you-- you remembered him. And he was very methodical and--

INT: Thank you very much.

And to conclude this interview on a positive note, would you like to speak about this photograph?

TR: This photograph was taken in 1995, when the members of the 45 Aid Society presented this Sefer Torah to the Borehamwood shul, in memory of their parents and families who perished in the Holocaust. This meant an awful lot to my husband and myself, who worked very hard in order to-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] to-- to have it. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

It was attend-- the [NON-ENGLISH] Sefer Torah was attended by very many people. And they now have a plaque in the shul which has each individual member who donated to the Sefer Torah in-- on the name of their parents. And they want to come together once a year and make a memorial day. And it means an awful lot to everybody, to all the boys.

INT: Thank you, again.